\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Guiding center Fokker-Planck equation
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

in the zero-banana width approximation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
J.
 Decker, Y.
 Peysson and A.
 Brizard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
05.02.2009
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The objective is to derive the reduced 3-D Fokker-Planck equation neglecting
 all particle drifts, meaning that the particle is assumed to remain on
 a given flux-surface.
 This operation can be conducted starting from the reduced 4-D guiding center
 Fokker-Planck equation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

 and taking the limit where terms of order 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{B}=\rho/L_{B}$
\end_inset

 are neglected but terms of order 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho/L_{n,T}$
\end_inset

 are kept, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is the Larmor radius, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{B}$
\end_inset

 is the magnetic gradients scale length and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{n,T}$
\end_inset

 is the temperature and density gradients scale length.
 Thus, classical radial transport terms will be kept, thus allowing a proper
 3D conservative formulation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08a"

\end_inset

 and setting the ground for the inclusion of anomalous radial transport
 in LUKE
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dec08"

\end_inset

.
 However, consistent derivation of neoclassical effects such as the bootstrap
 current will be lost.
 The same equation could be derived from other approaches [Bernstein & Molvig,
 Zaitsev] but the formalism of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

 is purposedly followed to prepare the ground for LUKE 2 where drift effects
 will be included.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Transformed Fokker-Planck equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The transformed Fokker-Planck equation for the 4-D guiding center distribution
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle F\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

-(17) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d_{R}}{dt}\left\langle F\right\rangle \left(Z_{R},t;J\right)=C_{R}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]\left(Z_{R},t;J\right)\label{redFP}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{R}$
\end_inset

 is the reduced coordinate system and 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 is the gyro-action.
 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{R}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]$
\end_inset

 is the reduced collision operator.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Poisson bracket in reduced coordinate system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 are functions of the guiding center coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{X},\varepsilon,\mu,\varphi)$
\end_inset

, the Poisson bracket of F and Gis given by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

-(39)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\left\{ F,G\right\} _{\text{gc}} & =\frac{\Omega}{B}\left[\frac{\partial F}{\partial\varphi}\left(\frac{\partial G}{\partial\mu}+B\frac{\partial G}{\partial\varepsilon}\right)-\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial\mu}+B\frac{\partial F}{\partial\varepsilon}\right)\frac{\partial G}{\partial\varphi}\right]\\
 & +\mathbf{v}_{gc}\mathbf{\cdot}\left(\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}F\frac{\partial G}{\partial\varepsilon}-\frac{\partial F}{\partial\varepsilon}\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}G\right)-\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{qB_{\Vert}^{\star}}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}F\times\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}G\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}=\nabla+\left[\mathbf{R}+\left(\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{2}\cdot\nabla\times\hat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\hat{\mathbf{b}}\right]\frac{\partial}{\partial\varphi}\]

\end_inset

and the definitions of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\Vert}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{gc}$
\end_inset

 are found in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

.
 In the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{B}=\rho/L_{B}\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 we have the following simplifications
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}B_{\Vert}^{\star} & \rightarrow & B\\
\mathbf{v}_{gc} & \rightarrow & v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}\end{aligned}
\label{limit}\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v_{\Vert}\right|=\sqrt{(2/m)(\varepsilon-\mu B)}$
\end_inset

 and the Jacobian of the transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})\longrightarrow(\mathbf{X},\varepsilon,\mu,\varphi)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{J=}\frac{mB}{\left|v_{\Vert}\right|}\end{equation}

\end_inset

In this limit, we also obtain simplified expressions for the gyroradius
 vector abd GC momentum
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\mathbf{\rho}_{\epsilon} & \rightarrow & \mathbf{\rho}_{0}=\frac{v_{\bot}}{\Omega}=\frac{1}{q}\sqrt{2\mu m/B}\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\\
\mathbf{p}_{\epsilon} & \rightarrow & m\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\Omega\frac{\partial\mathbf{\rho}_{0}}{\partial\varphi}\right)=m\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\end{aligned}
\label{gcvec}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Equations of Motion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recalling the Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
H=\varepsilon\end{equation}

\end_inset

We have then
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ F,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=\Omega\frac{\partial F}{\partial\varphi}+\mathbf{v}_{gc}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\nabla}^{\star}F\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus, defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{b}}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\hat{s}}\equiv\sin\varsigma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{b}}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\hat{z}}\equiv\cos\varsigma$
\end_inset

 (note that here any value 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq\alpha<2\pi$
\end_inset

 is possible) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\overset{\cdot}{\mathbf{X}} & = & \left\{ \mathbf{X},H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}\\
\overset{\cdot}{\psi} & = & \left\{ \psi,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=0\\
\overset{\cdot}{s} & = & \left\{ \theta,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=\sin\varsigma v_{\Vert}\\
\overset{\cdot}{z} & = & \left\{ z,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=\frac{1}{\Upsilon}\cos\varsigma v_{\Vert}\\
\overset{\cdot}{\varepsilon} & = & \left\{ \varepsilon,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=0\\
\overset{\cdot}{\mu} & = & \left\{ \mu,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=0\\
\overset{\cdot}{\varphi} & = & \left\{ \varphi,H\right\} _{\text{gc}}=\Omega+v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\left[\mathbf{R}+\left(\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{2}\cdot\nabla\times\hat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\hat{\mathbf{b}}\right]\simeq\Omega\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

which gives the following Vlasov part of the guiding center kinetic equation
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d_{R}}{dt}\left\langle F\right\rangle =\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial t}+v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}\left\langle F\right\rangle \end{equation}

\end_inset

or equivelently, with 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle F\right\rangle =\left\langle F\right\rangle \left(t,\psi,s,z,\varepsilon,\mu\right)$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d_{R}}{dt}\left\langle F\right\rangle =\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial t}+\sin\varsigma v_{\Vert}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial s}+\frac{1}{\Upsilon}\cos\varsigma v_{\Vert}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial z}\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using axisymmetry 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d_{R}}{dt}\left\langle F\right\rangle =\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial t}+\sin\varsigma v_{\Vert}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial s}\end{equation}

\end_inset

We see that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\psi,\varepsilon,\mu)$
\end_inset

 are invariants in the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{B}\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Guiding center Fokker-Planck collision operator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The guiding center Fokker-Planck collision operator is given by
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

-(17) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}_{\text{gcFP}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]=-\frac{1}{\mathcal{J}}\frac{\partial}{\partial Z^{\alpha}}\left[\mathcal{J}\left(\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\alpha}\right\rangle \left\langle F\right\rangle -\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\alpha\beta}\right\rangle \frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial Z^{\beta}}\right)\right]\label{Cfp}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the gyroangle-averaged drag and diffusion phase-space components are
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "gcvec"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

-(48-56) 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{aligned}\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \mathbf{K}_{\epsilon}\right\rangle \times\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{m\Omega}\\
\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \mathbf{K}_{\epsilon}\cdot\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\right\rangle \\
\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \sqrt{2\mu/(mB)}\mathbf{K}_{\epsilon}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right\rangle \end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{aligned}\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{XX}}\right\rangle  & =-\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{m\Omega}\times\left\langle \mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\right\rangle \times\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{m\Omega}\\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\varepsilon\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\cdot\mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\cdot\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\right\rangle \\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \sqrt{2\mu/(mB)}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\cdot\mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\cdot\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\right\rangle \\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu\mu}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle \frac{2\mu}{mB}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\cdot\mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right\rangle \\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{m\Omega}\times\left\langle \mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\cdot\left(v_{\Vert}\hat{\mathbf{b}}+\sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right)\right\rangle \\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}\mu}\right\rangle  & =\frac{\hat{\mathbf{b}}}{m\Omega}\times\left\langle \sqrt{2\mu B/m}\mathbb{D}_{\epsilon}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\bot}}\right\rangle \end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Isotropic field-particle model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the case of isotropic field-particle distributions these components reduce
 in the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{B}\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 to
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bri04"

\end_inset

-(48-56) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\left\langle \mathbf{K}_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}}\right\rangle  & =0\\
\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =-2\nu_{\epsilon}\varepsilon\\
\left\langle K_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu}\right\rangle  & =-2\nu_{\epsilon}\mu\end{aligned}
\label{Kiso}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\left\langle \mathbb{D}_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{XX}}\right\rangle  & =\frac{B}{m\Omega^{2}}\left(\mathcal{D}_{\mu}+\frac{D_{\bot\epsilon}}{mB}\right)\mathbb{I}_{\bot}\\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\varepsilon\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =2\mathcal{D}_{\varepsilon}\varepsilon\\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =2\mathcal{D}_{\varepsilon}\mu\\
\left\langle D_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mu\mu}\right\rangle  & =2\mu\left(2\mathcal{D}_{\mu}+\frac{D_{\bot\epsilon}}{mB}\right)\\
\left\langle \mathbf{D}_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}\varepsilon}\right\rangle  & =0\\
\left\langle \mathbf{D}_{\textrm{gc}}^{\mathbf{X}\mu}\right\rangle  & =0\end{aligned}
\label{Diso}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{aligned}\mathcal{D}_{\varepsilon} & =\frac{D_{\Vert\epsilon}}{m}\\
\mathcal{D}_{\mu} & =\mu\frac{\left(D_{\Vert\epsilon}-D_{\bot\epsilon}\right)}{2m\varepsilon}\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset

and the following expressions for the drag and diffusion coefficients were
 used 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{aligned}\mathbf{K} & =-\nu\mathbf{p}\\
\mathbb{D} & =D_{\Vert\epsilon}\frac{\mathbf{\mathbf{p}p}}{p^{2}}+D_{\bot\epsilon}\left(\mathbb{I}-\frac{\mathbf{\mathbf{p}p}}{p^{2}}\right)\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inserting (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Kiso"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Diso"

\end_inset

) in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Cfp"

\end_inset

) yields
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathcal{C}_{\text{gcFP}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]=\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{p}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]+\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{X}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]\]

\end_inset

with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{p}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]= & 2\left|v_{\Vert}\right|\frac{\partial}{\partial\varepsilon}\left[\frac{1}{\left|v_{\Vert}\right|}\left(\nu_{\epsilon}\varepsilon\left\langle F\right\rangle +\frac{D_{\Vert\epsilon}}{m}\left[\varepsilon\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\varepsilon}+\mu\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\mu}\right]\right)\right]\\
 & +2\left|v_{\Vert}\right|\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu}\left[\frac{\mu}{\left|v_{\Vert}\right|}\left(\nu_{\epsilon}\left\langle F\right\rangle +\frac{D_{\Vert\epsilon}}{m}\left(\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\varepsilon}+\frac{\mu}{\varepsilon}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\mu}\right)+\frac{D_{\bot\epsilon}}{mB}\left(1-\frac{\mu B}{\varepsilon}\right)\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\mu}\right)\right]\end{aligned}
\label{Cfpisop}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{X}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]=\frac{\left|v_{\Vert}\right|}{q\Omega}\nabla\cdot\left[\frac{1}{\left|v_{\Vert}\right|}\left(\frac{\mu D_{\Vert\epsilon}}{2m\varepsilon}+\frac{D_{\bot\epsilon}}{mB}\left(1-\frac{\mu B}{2\varepsilon}\right)\right)\mathbb{I}_{\bot}\cdot\nabla\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]\label{Cfpisox}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Collision operator in 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{X},p,\xi_{0})$
\end_inset

 coordinates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Momentum space dynamics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\varepsilon & = & \frac{p^{2}}{2m}\\
\mu & = & \frac{p^{2}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)}{2mB_{0}}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 which is inverted as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
p & = & \sqrt{p_{\Vert}^{2}+p_{\bot}^{2}}=\sqrt{2m\varepsilon}\\
\xi_{0} & = & \frac{p_{\Vert0}}{p}=\sqrt{1-\frac{\mu B_{0}}{\varepsilon}}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We see that 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $(p,\xi_{0},\psi)$
\end_inset

 are also invariants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then, the transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $(\varepsilon,\mu)\longrightarrow(p,\xi_{0})$
\end_inset

 is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\partial/\partial\varepsilon\\
\partial/\partial\mu\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\partial p/\partial\varepsilon & \partial\xi_{0}/\partial\varepsilon\\
\partial p/\partial\mu & \partial\xi_{0}/\partial\mu\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\partial/\partial p\\
\partial/\partial\xi_{0}\end{array}\right)=\mathbb{L}^{-1}\mathbb{\cdot}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\partial/\partial p\\
\partial/\partial\xi_{0}\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with the transformation matrix 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{L}^{-1}\equiv\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\partial p/\partial\varepsilon & \partial\xi_{0}/\partial\varepsilon\\
\partial p/\partial\mu & \partial\xi_{0}/\partial\mu\end{array}\right)=\frac{m}{p^{2}\xi_{0}}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
p\xi_{0} & 1-\xi_{0}^{2}\\
0 & -B_{0}\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

Defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi=B/B_{0}$
\end_inset

 and using 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $v_{\Vert}=p\xi/m$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\xi=\sigma\sqrt{1-\Psi\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)}\]

\end_inset

the following expression is obtained (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Cfpisop"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{p}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]= & \frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial}{\partial p}\left[p^{3}\left(\nu_{\epsilon}\left\langle F\right\rangle +\frac{D_{\Vert\epsilon}}{m}\left[\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\varepsilon}+\frac{\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)}{B_{0}}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\mu}\right]\right)\right]\\
 & -\frac{\xi}{\xi_{0}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi_{0}}\left[\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)\frac{\xi D_{\bot\epsilon}}{mB}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\mu}\right]\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which transforms into 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{p}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial}{\partial p}\left[p^{2}\left(p\nu_{\epsilon}\left\langle F\right\rangle +D_{\Vert\epsilon}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial p}\right)\right]+\frac{1}{\Psi p^{2}}\frac{\xi}{\xi_{0}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi_{0}}\left[\frac{\xi}{\xi_{0}}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)D_{\bot\epsilon}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\xi_{0}}\right]\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Configuration space dynamics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The operator (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Cfpisox"

\end_inset

) transforms into 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{X}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]=\frac{\xi}{B}\nabla\cdot\left[\frac{B_{0}}{\xi}D_{X\epsilon}\mathbb{I}_{\bot}\cdot\nabla\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]\label{Cfpisox2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we define
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{X\epsilon}=\frac{1}{2m^{2}\Omega_{0}^{2}}\left(\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)D_{\Vert\epsilon}+\left[\frac{2}{\Psi}-\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)\right]D_{\bot\epsilon}\right)\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Collision operator in 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\xi_{0},\psi,s,z)$
\end_inset

 coordinates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using axisymmetry, the operator (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Cfpisox3"

\end_inset

) becomes
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{X}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]= & \frac{\xi}{B}\frac{\left\Vert \nabla\psi\right\Vert }{\Upsilon}\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\left[\frac{\Upsilon B_{0}}{\xi}D_{X\epsilon}\hat{\mathbf{\psi}}\cdot\mathbb{I}_{\bot}\cdot\left(\nabla\psi\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\psi}+\mathbf{\hat{s}}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial s}\right)\right]\\
 & +\frac{\xi}{B}\frac{\left\Vert \nabla\psi\right\Vert }{\Upsilon}\frac{\partial}{\partial s}\left[\frac{\Upsilon B_{0}}{\left\Vert \nabla\psi\right\Vert \xi}D_{X\epsilon}\mathbb{I}_{\bot}\cdot\left(\nabla\psi\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial\psi}+\mathbf{\hat{s}}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial s}\right)\right]\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Bounce-averaged guiding center Fokker Planck equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The guiding center Fokker-Planck equation for 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle F\right\rangle (p,\xi_{0},\psi,s)$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 is now
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial t}+\sin\varsigma\frac{p\xi}{m}\frac{\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle }{\partial s}=\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{X}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]+\mathcal{C}_{\mathbf{p}}\left[\left\langle F\right\rangle \right]\end{equation}

\end_inset

In the low collisionality regime, this equation gives 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\partial\left\langle F\right\rangle /\partial s$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle F\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is constant along a field line and symmetric in the trapped region.
 We define then the bounce-averaging operation
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\frac{1}{\lambda}\oint\frac{ds}{\sin\varsigma}\frac{\xi_{0}}{\xi}\mathcal{A}\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda=\oint\frac{ds}{\sin\varsigma}\frac{\xi_{0}}{\xi}\]

\end_inset

and apply it to () 
